[{"location":"metrics/faithfulness/#Faithfulness","page":"Faithfulness","title":"Faithfulness","text":"Faithfulness metrics are planned in this category. Current package versions do not yet expose public faithfulness metric types.","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/getting_started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","title":"Getting Started","text":"This guide walks through a complete first workflow with XAIMetrics.jl:\n\ndefine a model and explanation method,\nprepare batched input,\nevaluate robustness metrics,\ncustomize perturbation and normalization behavior.\n\nnote: Note\nXAIMetrics.jl evaluates explanations generated by ExplainableAI.jl methods.","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/getting_started/#Prerequisites","page":"Getting Started","title":"Prerequisites","text":"using XAIMetrics\nusing ExplainableAI\nusing Flux\nusing Random\n\nCreate a simple classifier and an explanation method:\n\nmodel = Chain(\n    Flux.flatten,\n    Dense(28 * 28, 64, relu),\n    Dense(64, 10),\n)\n\nmethod = InputTimesGradient(model)","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/getting_started/#Prepare-input-and-targets","page":"Getting Started","title":"Prepare input and targets","text":"evaluate expects batched input with batch dimension last. For image data in Flux, this is typically WHCN.\n\nbatch_size = 8\nx = rand(Float32, 28, 28, 1, batch_size)\ny = rand(1:10, batch_size)\n\nnote: Input shape\nThe last dimension is treated as batch dimension by XAIMetrics.jl.","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/getting_started/#First-metric-evaluation","page":"Getting Started","title":"First metric evaluation","text":"Start with AvgSensitivity:\n\nmetric = AvgSensitivity(\n    nr_samples = 30,\n    perturb_config = PerturbationConfig(\n        uniform_noise!;\n        params = (; lower = 0.05, upper = 0.1),\n    ),\n)\n\nscores = evaluate(metric, method, x; y = y)\n\nscores is a vector with one score per sample in the batch.\n\nThen continue with LocalLipschitzEstimate:\n\nlle_metric = LocalLipschitzEstimate(\n    nr_samples = 50,\n    perturb_config = PerturbationConfig(\n        gaussian_perturbation!;\n        params = (; std = 0.2),\n    ),\n    return_nan_when_prediction_changes = false,\n)\n\nlle_scores = evaluate(lle_metric, method, x; y = y)","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/getting_started/#Configuring-behavior","page":"Getting Started","title":"Configuring behavior","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/getting_started/#Perturbations","page":"Getting Started","title":"Perturbations","text":"cfg_uniform = PerturbationConfig(\n    uniform_noise!;\n    params = (; lower = 0.03, upper = 0.08))","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/getting_started/#Normalization","page":"Getting Started","title":"Normalization","text":"norm_cfg = NormalizationConfig(\n    normalize = true,\n    normalize_func = normalize_by_max_abs,\n)\n\nmetric_norm = AvgSensitivity(\n    nr_samples = 30,\n    perturb_config = cfg_uniform,\n    normalize_config = norm_cfg,\n)","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/getting_started/#Inspecting-perturbations","page":"Getting Started","title":"Inspecting perturbations","text":"You can also apply perturbations directly to a small input and print the result.\n\nRandom.seed!(7)\n\nx_demo = Float32[0.2, -0.4, 0.8]\nx_demo_pert = similar(x_demo)\n\ndemo_pert_cfg = PerturbationConfig(uniform_noise!; params = (; lower = 0.03, upper = 0.08))\nperturb_input!(x_demo_pert, x_demo, demo_pert_cfg)\n\nprintln(\"x_demo          = \", x_demo)\nprintln(\"x_demo_pert     = \", x_demo_pert)\nprintln(\"perturbation Δ  = \", x_demo_pert .- x_demo)","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/getting_started/#Inspecting-normalization","page":"Getting Started","title":"Inspecting normalization","text":"Normalization can be inspected the same way.\n\na_demo = Float32[-2.0, 0.0, 1.0, 4.0]\na_norm = normalize_by_max_abs(a_demo)\n\nprintln(\"a_demo                  = \", a_demo)\nprintln(\"normalize_by_max_abs    = \", a_norm)\nprintln(\"maximum(abs, a_demo)    = \", maximum(abs, a_demo))\nprintln(\"maximum(abs, a_norm)    = \", maximum(abs, a_norm))","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/getting_started/#Handling-prediction-changes","page":"Getting Started","title":"Handling prediction changes","text":"Both robustness metrics support return_nan_when_prediction_changes:\n\ntrue: keep changed-prediction cases as NaN\nfalse: aggregate over available finite scores\n\nThis is useful when you want to separate explanation instability from class-change effects.","category":"section"},{"location":"generated/getting_started/#Next-steps","page":"Getting Started","title":"Next steps","text":"Robustness metric reference: Robustness\nConfiguration overview: Configurations\nFunction-level APIs: Normalizations, Perturbations, Similarities\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","category":"section"},{"location":"metrics/localisation/#Localisation","page":"Localisation","title":"Localisation","text":"Localisation metrics are planned in this category. Current package versions do not yet expose public localisation metric types.","category":"section"},{"location":"metrics/randomisation/#Randomisation","page":"Randomisation","title":"Randomisation","text":"Randomisation metrics are planned in this category. Current package versions do not yet expose public randomisation metric types.","category":"section"},{"location":"metrics/axiomatic/#Axiomatic","page":"Axiomatic","title":"Axiomatic","text":"Axiomatic metrics are planned in this category. Current package versions do not yet expose public axiomatic metric types.","category":"section"},{"location":"configurations/#Configurations","page":"Configurations","title":"Configurations","text":"Configuration objects make metric behavior explicit and reusable.","category":"section"},{"location":"configurations/#Normalization","page":"Configurations","title":"Normalization","text":"Normalization controls whether and how explanation values are scaled before metric computation.\n\nMain config type: NormalizationConfig\nTypical use: max-absolute scaling for comparability across samples\nFull API and functions: Normalizations","category":"section"},{"location":"configurations/#Perturbation","page":"Configurations","title":"Perturbation","text":"Perturbation defines how noisy/modified inputs are generated during robustness evaluation.\n\nTypical use: choose perturbation function and distribution parameters\nFunction-level API: Perturbations","category":"section"},{"location":"configurations/#Similarity","page":"Configurations","title":"Similarity","text":"Similarity defines how differences between original and perturbed explanations are measured.\n\nMain config type: SimilarityConfig\nTypical use: select numerator/denominator norms and similarity function\nFull API and helper functions: Similarities","category":"section"},{"location":"configurations/#Where-to-continue","page":"Configurations","title":"Where to continue","text":"For concrete constructor examples, see Getting Started.\nFor function-level details, view Normalizations, Perturbations, and Similarities.","category":"section"},{"location":"configurations/#XAIMetrics.NormalizationConfig","page":"Configurations","title":"XAIMetrics.NormalizationConfig","text":"NormalizationConfig(; normalize=true, normalize_func=normalize_by_max_abs)\n\nConfiguration for explanation normalization in metric pipelines.\n\nnormalize: enables/disables normalization\nnormalize_func: callable applied to explanation batches when normalization is enabled\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"configurations/#XAIMetrics.normalize_explanations-Tuple{AbstractArray, NormalizationConfig}","page":"Configurations","title":"XAIMetrics.normalize_explanations","text":"normalize_explanations(a_batch, config)\n\nApply normalization to an explanation batch according to config. Returns the original batch unchanged when config.normalize == false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"configurations/#XAIMetrics.PerturbationConfig-Tuple{Function}","page":"Configurations","title":"XAIMetrics.PerturbationConfig","text":"PerturbationConfig()\n\nCreates a configuration for perturbation logic.\n\nExamples\n\nusing XAIMetrics\nusing Distributions\n\nx = rand(10)\nx_gaussian = similar(x)\nx_uniform = similar(x)\n\n# Setup configurations\ncfg_gaussian = PerturbationConfig(gaussian_perturbation!; params=(; std=0.1))\ncfg_uniform = PerturbationConfig(uniform_noise!; params=(; lower=5))\n\n# Apply perturbations\nperturb_input!(x_gaussian, x, cfg_gaussian)\nperturb_input!(x_uniform, x, cfg_uniform)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"configurations/#XAIMetrics.perturb_input!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}, PerturbationConfig}} where T","page":"Configurations","title":"XAIMetrics.perturb_input!","text":"perturb_input!(x_perturbed, x_batch, config)\n\nApply in-place perturbation to x_batch using the perturbation function and distribution stored in config.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"configurations/#XAIMetrics.SimilarityConfig","page":"Configurations","title":"XAIMetrics.SimilarityConfig","text":"SimilarityConfig(; similarity_func=lipschitz_constant, norm_numerator=distance_manhattan, norm_denominator=distance_euclidean, kwargs=Dict())\n\nConfiguration for similarity computation between original and perturbed explanations/inputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"configurations/#XAIMetrics.compute_similarity-Tuple{SimilarityConfig, Vararg{AbstractMatrix, 4}}","page":"Configurations","title":"XAIMetrics.compute_similarity","text":"compute_similarity(config, A_orig, A_perturbed, X_orig, X_perturbed)\n\nDispatches to the similarity function defined in the config.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"perturbations/#Function-Perturbations","page":"Functions: Perturbations","title":"Function Perturbations","text":"Perturbation functions are in-place helpers used by PerturbationConfig. They sample additive noise from a Distributions.Sampleable object.","category":"section"},{"location":"perturbations/#XAIMetrics.gaussian_perturbation!-Tuple{Any, Any, Distributions.Sampleable}","page":"Functions: Perturbations","title":"XAIMetrics.gaussian_perturbation!","text":"gaussian_perturbation!(x_perturbed, x_batch, distribution)\n\nSample additive Gaussian-like noise from distribution into x_perturbed and return x_batch .+ noise in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"perturbations/#XAIMetrics.uniform_noise!-Tuple{Any, Any, Distributions.Sampleable}","page":"Functions: Perturbations","title":"XAIMetrics.uniform_noise!","text":"uniform_noise!(x_perturbed, x_batch, distribution)\n\nSample additive uniform-like noise from distribution into x_perturbed and return x_batch .+ noise in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"normalizations/#Function-Normalizations","page":"Functions: Normalizations","title":"Function Normalizations","text":"Normalization utilities control value scaling and numerically stable division.","category":"section"},{"location":"normalizations/#XAIMetrics.normalize_by_max_abs-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Functions: Normalizations","title":"XAIMetrics.normalize_by_max_abs","text":"normalize_by_max_abs(a::AbstractArray)\nnormalize_by_max_abs(a::AbstractArray, dims)\n\nNormalize an array by dividing by its maximum absolute value, scaling values to [-1, 1]. Optionally, normalization can be performed along specified dimensions dims.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> A = [1.0 -2.0; 3.0 -4.0]\n2×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n 1.0  -2.0\n 3.0  -4.0\n\njulia> normalize_by_max_abs(A)\n2×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n 0.25  -0.5\n 0.75  -1.0\n\njulia> normalize_by_max_abs(A, 1)\n2×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n 0.333333  -0.5\n 1.0       -1.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"normalizations/#XAIMetrics.stable_division-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T}, AbstractArray{T}}} where T","page":"Functions: Normalizations","title":"XAIMetrics.stable_division","text":"stable_division(numerator, denominator)\n\nPerform division with an epsilon-stabilized denominator to reduce division-by-zero instabilities for scalar and array inputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"metrics/_overview/#Metrics","page":"Overview","title":"Metrics","text":"XAIMetrics.jl organizes metrics into six categories:\n\nAxiomatic\nComplexity\nFaithfulness\nLocalisation\nRandomisation\nRobustness\n\nCurrently implemented metric types are documented under Robustness.","category":"section"},{"location":"metrics/_overview/#Evaluation-entry-point","page":"Overview","title":"Evaluation entry point","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"metrics/_overview/#XAIMetrics.evaluate","page":"Overview","title":"XAIMetrics.evaluate","text":"evaluate(...)\n\nInternal function to compute the Local Lipschitz Estimate for a batch of data.\n\n\n\n\n\nevaluate(...)\n\nInternal function to compute the AvgSensitivity for a batch of data.\n\n\n\n\n\nevaluate(metric, method, x; y, s)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"metrics/complexity/#Complexity","page":"Complexity","title":"Complexity","text":"Complexity metrics are planned in this category. Current package versions do not yet expose public complexity metric types.","category":"section"},{"location":"metrics/robustness/#Robustness","page":"Robustness","title":"Robustness","text":"Robustness metrics evaluate how stable explanations are under small perturbations.","category":"section"},{"location":"metrics/robustness/#Local-Lipschitz-Estimate","page":"Robustness","title":"Local Lipschitz Estimate","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"metrics/robustness/#Average-Sensitivity","page":"Robustness","title":"Average Sensitivity","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"metrics/robustness/#Typical-settings","page":"Robustness","title":"Typical settings","text":"nr_samples: number of perturbation samples per input\nperturb_config: perturbation distribution and scale/range\nnormalize_config: optional normalization before comparison\nreturn_nan_when_prediction_changes: optional masking when predictions flip","category":"section"},{"location":"metrics/robustness/#XAIMetrics.LocalLipschitzEstimate","page":"Robustness","title":"XAIMetrics.LocalLipschitzEstimate","text":"LocalLipschitzEstimate(; nr_samples=200, similarity_func, norm_numerator, norm_denominator, perturb_std=0.1, return_nan_when_prediction_changes=false)\n<: AbstractRobustnessMetric\n\nRobustness metric that tests the consistency in the explanation for neighboring examples by estimating the local Lipschitz constant.\n\nTODO: add more perturb functions, add normalization functions\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"metrics/robustness/#XAIMetrics.AvgSensitivity","page":"Robustness","title":"XAIMetrics.AvgSensitivity","text":"AvgSensitivity\n\nCompute the average sensitivity of a model with respect to its input features.\n\nThis metric measures how much the model's output changes on average, when each feature of the input is slightly perturbed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"similarities/#Function-Similarities","page":"Functions: Similarities","title":"Function Similarities","text":"Similarity functions define how explanation differences are quantified. They are used directly by metrics and by SimilarityConfig.","category":"section"},{"location":"similarities/#XAIMetrics.difference-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Functions: Similarities","title":"XAIMetrics.difference","text":"difference(a, b)\n\nCalculate the difference between two images or explanations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"similarities/#XAIMetrics.distance_euclidean-Tuple{AbstractArray, AbstractArray}","page":"Functions: Similarities","title":"XAIMetrics.distance_euclidean","text":"distance_euclidean(a::AbstractArray, b::AbstractArray)\n\nCalculate Euclidean distance between two arrays (e.g., images or explanations).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"similarities/#XAIMetrics.distance_manhattan-Tuple{AbstractArray, AbstractArray}","page":"Functions: Similarities","title":"XAIMetrics.distance_manhattan","text":"distance_manhattan(a::AbstractArray, b::AbstractArray)\n\nCalculate Manhattan (L1) distance between two arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"similarities/#XAIMetrics.lipschitz_constant-NTuple{4, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Functions: Similarities","title":"XAIMetrics.lipschitz_constant","text":"lipschitz_constant(a, b, c, d; norm_numerator, norm_denominator)\n\nCompute the batched local Lipschitz constant for a set of explanations and inputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"similarities/#XAIMetrics.sensitivity_ratio-Union{Tuple{T}, NTuple{4, AbstractMatrix{T}}} where T","page":"Functions: Similarities","title":"XAIMetrics.sensitivity_ratio","text":"sensitivity_ratio(A_orig, A_perturbed, X_orig, X_perturbed; norm_numerator, norm_denominator, kwargs...)\n\nComputes the ratio norm_numerator(A_orig - A_perturbed) / norm_denominator(A_orig).\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"method"},{"location":"#XAIMetrics.jl","page":"Home","title":"XAIMetrics.jl","text":"XAIMetrics.jl provides evaluation metrics for explanation methods in Julia. The current baseline focuses on robustness metrics and reusable configuration objects.","category":"section"},{"location":"#Installation","page":"Home","title":"Installation","text":"To install this package and its dependencies, open the Julia REPL and run\n\njulia> ]add  XAIMetrics # not yet as the package is not registered","category":"section"},{"location":"#What-is-available-now","page":"Home","title":"What is available now","text":"Metric entry point: evaluate\nRobustness metrics: LocalLipschitzEstimate, AvgSensitivity\nConfigurations: NormalizationConfig, PerturbationConfig\nUtility functions: normalization, perturbation, and similarity helpers","category":"section"},{"location":"#Manual","page":"Home","title":"Manual","text":"Pages = [\n    \"generated/getting_started.md\"\n]\nDepth = 2","category":"section"},{"location":"#API-References","page":"Home","title":"API References","text":"Pages = [\n    \"configurations.md\",\n    \"normalizations.md\",\n    \"perturbations.md\",\n    \"similarities.md\"\n]\nDepth = 2","category":"section"},{"location":"#Metrics","page":"Home","title":"Metrics","text":"Pages = [\n    \"metrics/robustness.md\"\n]\nDepth = 2","category":"section"}]
